<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Contents/Info.plist for WSL Commentary. Owner: WP-6 (macOS port).

  This file REPLACES the one Wails would otherwise generate. Wails' packager
  reads build/darwin/Info.plist from the project when it exists and only falls
  back to its own embedded template when it does not (buildassets.ReadFile —
  and note that the fallback WRITES the template here, so an accidental
  deletion of this file looks like it worked and silently reintroduces every
  problem below). It is still run through text/template with .Name, .Info and
  .OutputFilename, so the values that belong to wails.json are still spelled
  as placeholders and there is exactly one place to bump a version.

  THREE THINGS THE STOCK TEMPLATE GETS WRONG FOR THIS APP, in order of how
  expensive they are to find out the hard way:

  1. NO NSMicrophoneUsageDescription. This is not a missing nicety. When a
     process first touches CoreAudio input and TCC finds no usage string in
     its bundle, macOS does not deny the request and it does not prompt — it
     TERMINATES THE PROCESS. To the operator that is the app vanishing the
     instant they press Start. It is also invisible in every test that stops
     short of real capture, which is most of them.

     It covers both users of the microphone in this app, which are not the
     same code at all: osxaudiosrc in the GStreamer contribution pipeline, and
     getUserMedia inside the WKWebView (frontend/src/ui/app.js — the silent
     grant that makes enumerateDevices() return labelled devices). WKWebView
     capture is attributed to the HOST bundle, so one string serves both.

  2. CFBundleIdentifier is "com.wails." followed by the templated, sanitised
     project name — i.e. "com.wails.wslcomms". (The template call itself is
     described rather than quoted here: this file is still fed through
     text/template, so writing it out in a comment would execute it.)
     Wrong on two counts: it is a namespace this project
     does not own, and it is derived from wails.json's "name", so renaming the
     project would silently change the identity that TCC's microphone grant,
     the keychain ACL and the notarisation ticket are all keyed on. An
     operator who had already granted the microphone would be re-prompted, and
     the stored credential would be unreachable.

     So it is a LITERAL here, deliberately not a placeholder:
     tv.wslstudios.commentary. That is the identifier the signed builds on
     this machine already carry (codesign was being given -i to force it,
     which fixed the signature and left the plist lying), so nothing about
     existing grants changes. main.go's single-instance mutex uses a different
     reverse-DNS string (uk.co.wslstudios.wslcomms) and that is not touched
     from here — it is a flock name, not an OS-visible identity, and changing
     it is internal/… territory rather than the packager's.

  3. LSMinimumSystemVersion 10.13.0, which for this bundle is a lie by fifteen
     major versions. MEASURED on the staged tree, not assumed: Homebrew builds
     its bottles for the build machine's macOS major version, so every
     vendored dylib carries LC_BUILD_VERSION minos 26.0 —

       otool -l Contents/Frameworks/libgstreamer-1.0.0.dylib | grep -A1 minos
         minos 26.0

     — and dyld refuses to map them on anything older. The .app would install
     happily on macOS 14 and then fail to launch with a dyld message no
     operator can act on. build/bundle-gst-darwin.sh computes the real floor
     across the whole staged payload and writes MINOS-FLOOR into the bundle
     manifest; build/ship-darwin.sh refuses to ship when this key promises
     support below it. Lowering the floor is a matter of building GStreamer
     against an older SDK, not of editing this number.

  DELIBERATELY ABSENT, so that nobody adds them back "for completeness":

  * NSCameraUsageDescription — nothing in this product opens a camera. The two
    getUserMedia call sites both ask for { audio: true } and the pipeline has
    no video source at all: its video is a still PNG through
    filesrc ! pngdec ! imagefreeze. A usage string for a device the app never
    touches is a permission dialogue the operator has no way to interpret, and
    it widens what a compromised WebView could ask the OS for.

  * LSUIElement — this is an ordinary windowed application with a Dock icon.

  * NSAppTransportSecurity — the WebView loads its assets from the wails://
    scheme, not over the network. The remote-control listener is a SERVER this
    app runs; ATS governs outgoing NSURLSession traffic and has nothing to say
    about it.

  * NSSupportsAutomaticGraphicsSwitching — arm64 only. There is no second GPU
    to switch to.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
    <dict>
        <key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key>
        <string>en_GB</string>
        <key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
        <string>6.0</string>
        <key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
        <string>APPL</string>

        <key>CFBundleName</key>
        <string>{{.Info.ProductName}}</string>
        <key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
        <string>{{.Info.ProductName}}</string>
        <key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
        <string>{{.OutputFilename}}</string>

        <!-- Literal, never derived. See note 2 above. -->
        <key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
        <string>tv.wslstudios.commentary</string>

        <!-- Both from wails.json's info.productVersion, so a release is one
             edit. CFBundleVersion is the build identity Apple's notary service
             and the updater compare; CFBundleShortVersionString is what the
             About box shows. They are the same string here because this
             product has no separate build number, and ship-darwin.sh requires
             the version it was given to match this. -->
        <key>CFBundleVersion</key>
        <string>{{.Info.ProductVersion}}</string>
        <key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
        <string>{{.Info.ProductVersion}}</string>

        <key>CFBundleGetInfoString</key>
        <string>{{.Info.Comments}}</string>
        <key>NSHumanReadableCopyright</key>
        <string>{{.Info.Copyright}}</string>
        <key>CFBundleIconFile</key>
        <string>iconfile</string>

        <!-- 26.0, and it is measured rather than chosen. See note 3 above. -->
        <key>LSMinimumSystemVersion</key>
        <string>26.0</string>
        <key>LSApplicationCategoryType</key>
        <string>public.app-category.video</string>

        <key>NSHighResolutionCapable</key>
        <true/>
        <!-- Cocoa defaults to NSApplication when this is absent, which is why
             the stock template gets away without it. Stated anyway: this app
             creates an NSWindow, an NSView overlay sibling of the WKWebView,
             and does Cocoa work on the main thread while GStreamer runs on its
             own. None of that is safe under a different principal class, and
             an implicit default is a poor place for that to be recorded. -->
        <key>NSPrincipalClass</key>
        <string>NSApplication</string>

        <!-- MANDATORY. Its absence is a kill, not a denial. See note 1. -->
        <key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
        <string>WSL Commentary needs the microphone to send your commentary to the production gallery.</string>

        <!-- macOS 15 and later gate outbound connections to addresses on the
             local network behind their own TCC prompt, and the prompt shows
             this string. The app reaches an M2L-X over SRT, which is usually a
             cloud host and would not trigger it — but a facility running the
             switcher on-prem, or an operator pointed at a receiver in the same
             truck, is exactly the case that does, and the failure without this
             key is a connection that silently never establishes. Listening on
             80/443 for the remote-control page does not trigger it; connecting
             out does. -->
        <key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
        <string>WSL Commentary needs the local network to reach an M2L-X switcher or SRT receiver on the same site as you.</string>
    </dict>
</plist>
